      "extract": "In this section we recall the main points of the Burgess method in dimension 1, outline the difﬁculties that arise in n ≥2 dimensions, and then sketch how we overcome these difﬁculties to obtain our main theorem. At its heart, the Burgess method in the classical 1-dimensional setting builds from a character sum S(N; H) of length H < q, a character sum over a complete set of residues modulo q. Doing so by a Fourier",
